    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Faults are hydraulically complex structures. In many cases, the hydraulic behavior of a fault allows it be classified as a barrier, a conduit, a barrier-conduit, or a conduit-barrier. Each type of fault produces a characteristic perturbation in the flow field. Barriers are characterized by pressure support and have steep hydraulic gradients within the fault zone, while conduits have low pressure gradients within the fault and produce roughly circular head perturbations at their termination. Barrier-conduits and conduit-barriers display characteristics of both barriers and conduits. Faults can be considered hydraulically equivalent if they produce the same perturbation in the distribution of head. An analytical solution for idealized fault models indicates that the hydraulic behavior of conduits depends most strongly on fault width while the hydraulic behavior of barriers depends largely on fault permeability.\r\n\r\nIn the Molasse Basin, the hydraulic behavior of faults causes local perturbations in the flow field that scale with the scale of faulting, but the regional distribution of head is largely independent of faulting. Fluid pressures are typically used to understand basin scale flow systems. However, we have found that flow velocities are more sensitive to fault properties than head distributions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
